entry daughter-nuclide
term: Daughter Nuclide
related_terms: parent-nuclide
related_terms: decay
related_terms: decay-chain
related_terms: radioactivity
related_terms: radionuclide
quick_facts: The daughter is the nucleus the parent becomes
quick_facts: It is a different nucleus, so nothing simply vanishes
quick_facts: It can be stable or unstable
quick_facts: An unstable daughter decays again
quick_facts: A chain ends at a stable daughter

sources:
    {"organization":"OpenStax","title":"University Physics Volume 3: Radioactive Decay","url":"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/10-3-radioactive-decay"}

definition:
    The nucleus that is left after a radioactive decay. It is a different nucleus from the parent. The daughter can be stable, and then the changing stops, or it can be unstable too, and then it decays again. A run of decays like this is a decay chain.

debunking_snippet:
    “Every decay makes a stable atom.” The daughter can be unstable too, and then it decays again.
